RD('C1','Loss Aversion','Tâm lý sợ mất mát','⚖️','Behavioural economists have long observed that losses loom larger than gains of equal size. Losing fifty pounds typically stings more than finding fifty pounds pleases, a asymmetry that helps explain why investors cling to failing shares in the hope of breaking even. Had people behaved as classical models predicted, they would weigh the two outcomes identically. Instead, they tend to take irrational risks to avoid a certain loss while shying away from gambles that offer a comparable upside. Critics caution that the effect is not universal: it appears weaker when stakes are trivial, and some researchers contend that it has been overstated by laboratory designs. Nonetheless, the phenomenon has proved remarkably durable in explaining everyday conduct, from reluctance to cancel unused subscriptions to the stubborn attachment that owners develop toward possessions they have only recently acquired. Marketers exploit it shamelessly, framing offers as deadlines that must not be missed rather than as opportunities to be seized.','Con người thường đau vì mất mát hơn là vui vì được lợi tương đương, dù hiệu ứng này không phải lúc nào cũng mạnh.','According to the passage, why might investors hold on to failing shares?|*They hope to avoid realising a loss|They believe classical models|They find gains more painful than losses§What do critics say about loss aversion?|It disappears entirely in real markets|*It may be weaker when little is at stake|It was first observed in marketing§The word "shamelessly" suggests that marketers|*use the effect without any embarrassment|hide the effect from consumers|are unaware of the effect');
RD('C1','Nudges and Default Options','Cú hích và lựa chọn mặc định','👉','A nudge, in the vocabulary of policy design, is any feature of the environment that steers behaviour without forbidding options or significantly altering incentives. The most potent example is the default. When employees are enrolled automatically in a pension scheme, with the freedom to opt out, participation rates soar compared with schemes requiring active enrolment. The explanation lies not in coercion but in inertia: most people accept whatever is presented to them. Proponents hail nudges as a cost-effective form of libertarian paternalism, respecting autonomy while improving outcomes. Detractors, however, raise two objections. First, who decides which outcome is desirable, and might that judgement reflect the preferences of officials rather than citizens? Second, subtle manipulation may erode the very capacity for deliberate choice that democracies prize. Perhaps the fairest conclusion is that nudges are tools, neither virtuous nor sinister in themselves; their legitimacy depends on transparency and on whether those subject to them would endorse the goal if asked.','Cú hích, đặc biệt là lựa chọn mặc định, định hướng hành vi mà không cấm đoán, nhưng gây tranh cãi về tính chính đáng.','What explains the high participation in automatic enrolment schemes?|Employees are legally forced to join|*People tend to accept what is presented|Pension schemes offer larger incentives§What is the first objection raised by detractors?|Nudges are too expensive|Nudges remove all options|*Officials may impose their own preferences§The author concludes that nudges are|inherently harmful to democracy|*neutral tools whose legitimacy depends on context|more effective than financial incentives');
RD('C1','Hyperbolic Discounting','Chiết khấu kiểu hyperbol','⏳','Offered a choice between one hundred pounds today and one hundred and ten next week, many people grab the immediate sum. Offered the same choice between a date a year away and one a year and a week away, the majority wait. This reversal, known as hyperbolic discounting, reveals that our impatience is not constant but sharpens dramatically as a reward draws near. Such inconsistency sits uneasily with the tidy assumption that rational agents apply a steady discount rate to the future. It also accounts for a familiar pattern: we resolve to exercise, save or diet when the temptation is remote, only to capitulate when it materialises. Some economists treat the self as a succession of competing selves, with the present one routinely sabotaging the plans of its predecessor. Commitment devices, such as locking savings in an account that penalises withdrawals, are therefore attractive precisely because they bind our future weakness. They work not by changing our desires but by tying our own hands.','Con người thiếu kiên nhẫn hơn khi phần thưởng ở gần, nên cần các cơ chế cam kết để tự ràng buộc.','What does the first example illustrate?|People always prefer larger sums|*Preferences can reverse as a reward becomes imminent|Most people refuse to wait a week§Why are commitment devices attractive?|They alter our underlying desires|They increase interest rates|*They constrain our future lapses in self-control§The phrase "tying our own hands" means|*deliberately limiting our own freedom to act|working harder than necessary|losing control of our finances');
RD('C1','The Ship of Theseus','Con tàu của Theseus','⛵','According to an ancient puzzle, the ship in which Theseus sailed was preserved by the Athenians, its decaying planks replaced one by one. Eventually, not a single original timber remained. Was it still the same ship? Those who answer yes point to continuity of form and function: identity, they argue, survives gradual change. Others retort that identity requires material constituents, so the vessel became a replica long before the last plank was swapped. The puzzle grows more perplexing if a collector gathers the discarded planks and reassembles them. Now two ships compete for the title of the original. Philosophers have drawn on the conundrum to illuminate questions that extend well beyond maritime heritage, notably personal identity. Our cells are continually replaced, and our memories are reconstructed rather than stored intact, yet we regard ourselves as persisting. The puzzle suggests that sameness may be less a fact about the world than a convention we adopt to make sense of change.','Nghịch lý con tàu Theseus đặt câu hỏi về bản sắc qua thay đổi và gợi ý rằng sự đồng nhất có thể là một quy ước.','Why do some deny that the ship remains the same?|*They hold that identity depends on original material|They believe the Athenians destroyed it|They think the form changed completely§What complication arises with the collector?|The ship becomes impossible to repair|*A second ship can claim to be the original|The planks are found to be fake§The author implies that sameness may be|an objective property of matter|*partly a convention people adopt|irrelevant to philosophy');
RD('C1','The Stoic Temperament','Tính cách khắc kỷ','🏛️','Few ancient schools have enjoyed so vigorous a revival as Stoicism, now invoked by executives, athletes and self-help authors alike. Its core teaching is deceptively simple: distinguish what lies within our control, chiefly our judgements and actions, from what does not, such as reputation, illness or the conduct of others. Distress, the Stoics held, arises not from events themselves but from the opinions we form about them. Hence the practice of rehearsing misfortune in advance, so that its arrival provokes composure rather than panic. Yet the popular version often caricatures the doctrine as grim emotional suppression. The original philosophers did not recommend numbness; they sought to cultivate rational responses and to meet duties to community with diligence. Marcus Aurelius, who ruled an empire, wrote his private reflections as a reminder to himself, not as a manual for others. It is somewhat ironic that a philosophy devoted to humility should now be marketed as a recipe for peak performance.','Chủ nghĩa Khắc kỷ dạy phân biệt điều kiểm soát được với điều không, nhưng bản phổ biến hiện nay thường bóp méo nó.','According to the Stoics, distress is caused by|unavoidable external events|*the judgements we make about events|a lack of ambition§How does the author view the popular version of Stoicism?|As an accurate summary|*As a caricature of the original|As more demanding than the original§What is ironic, according to the passage?|Marcus Aurelius was an emperor|Stoics rehearsed misfortune|*A humble philosophy is sold as a route to success');
RD('C1','The Trolley Problem','Bài toán xe điện','🚃','Imagine a runaway trolley heading towards five workers. You can divert it onto a side track, where it will kill one person instead. Most respondents say that pulling the lever is permissible. Now suppose the only way to stop the trolley is to push a stranger from a bridge into its path. The arithmetic is identical, yet the majority recoil. Philosophers use such scenarios not to settle what we should do but to expose the principles, often unstated, that guide our intuitions. Consequentialists insist that outcomes alone matter, so the cases should be treated alike. Deontologists reply that there is a morally significant difference between redirecting a threat and using a person as a mere instrument. Critics of the method complain that the scenarios are so contrived that they tell us little about real dilemmas. Defenders counter that, much like a physicist idealising a frictionless surface, the artificiality is precisely what allows a single variable to be isolated.','Bài toán xe điện dùng tình huống giả định để làm lộ các nguyên tắc đạo đức ngầm, dù phương pháp này bị phê bình.','Why do philosophers use trolley scenarios?|To determine the correct legal answer|*To reveal the principles behind moral intuitions|To prove that most people are consequentialists§How do deontologists explain the difference between the cases?|They deny that anyone is harmed|*They distinguish redirecting a threat from using a person as a tool|They prefer to save more lives§The comparison with a physicist suggests that artificiality|*can be a methodological advantage|makes results unreliable|should be avoided in science');
RD('C1','The Gut Microbiome','Hệ vi sinh đường ruột','🦠','The human gut harbours trillions of microorganisms whose collective genes outnumber our own many times over. Once dismissed as passive passengers, they are now implicated in digestion, immune regulation and even, tentatively, mood. Studies in animals show that transplanting gut bacteria from one mouse to another can transfer certain traits, which has fuelled speculation about a gut-brain axis. However, extrapolating from rodents to people demands considerable caution, and the field has been plagued by headline claims that outpace the evidence. Much of the data is correlational: people with a particular condition may carry distinctive microbes, but it is seldom clear whether the bacteria cause the illness or merely reflect it. Clinical successes are real but narrow, most notably the use of faecal transplants to treat stubborn infections with Clostridioides difficile. The prudent view is that the microbiome is a genuinely important frontier, albeit one in which enthusiasm currently runs well ahead of understanding, and in which commercial products often promise more than they deliver.','Hệ vi sinh ruột quan trọng nhưng nhiều tuyên bố vượt xa bằng chứng; thành công lâm sàng còn hạn hẹp.','What is the main weakness of much microbiome data?|It comes only from human subjects|*It is correlational, so causation is unclear|It contradicts animal studies§Which clinical success does the passage mention?|Improving mood in patients|*Treating persistent Clostridioides difficile infections|Preventing all digestive disease§The author attitude to the field can best be described as|*cautiously optimistic|dismissive|uncritically enthusiastic');
RD('C1','Antibiotic Resistance','Kháng kháng sinh','💉','Antibiotics transformed medicine so thoroughly that we now take for granted procedures, from chemotherapy to joint replacement, that depend upon them. Yet bacteria evolve, and every course of treatment exerts selective pressure that favours the survivors. Resistance is thus not an aberration but an inevitable consequence of use, and it is hastened by overprescription, incomplete courses and the routine feeding of antibiotics to livestock. The predicament is compounded by economics: a drug taken for a week and then retired offers a far poorer return than one taken daily for life, so pharmaceutical companies have been reluctant to invest. Proposals to remedy this include paying firms for access to new drugs regardless of volume, a model sometimes likened to a subscription. Stewardship, meaning the careful restriction of use, will be pivotal, but it demands international cooperation, since a resistant strain emerging in one country will not respect borders. Without concerted action, minor infections may once again become life-threatening.','Kháng kháng sinh là hệ quả tất yếu của việc sử dụng; cần cải cách kinh tế và hợp tác quốc tế.','Why are companies reluctant to develop new antibiotics?|Bacteria cannot be studied easily|*Short-course drugs yield poor financial returns|Regulators forbid their sale§What does the author mean by stewardship?|Selling drugs to livestock farmers|*Careful restriction of antibiotic use|Developing drugs in several countries§What can be inferred about resistant strains?|They remain confined to hospitals|They only affect livestock|*They can spread internationally');
RD('C1','The Rehabilitation of Brutalism','Sự phục hồi của kiến trúc Brutalist','🏢','For decades, the raw concrete monuments of postwar Britain and Eastern Europe were objects of popular loathing. The term Brutalism, often assumed to imply brutality, in fact derives from the French for raw concrete, and the architects who embraced it intended honesty of materials and social ambition rather than aggression. Many of the buildings were conceived as civic promises: housing for the many, libraries and universities open to all. That their execution often fell short, through cost-cutting, poor maintenance and weather staining, tarnished the style by association. Lately, however, opinion has softened. Younger admirers, drawn by the sculptural boldness of these structures, have campaigned against demolition, and some have been granted protected status. Sceptics maintain that nostalgia is a luxury for those who do not have to live in damp, draughty flats. This tension is instructive: aesthetic judgement is seldom divorced from lived experience, and a building that photographs magnificently may nevertheless serve its occupants poorly.','Kiến trúc Brutalist từng bị ghét nhưng nay được đánh giá lại, dù vẫn có tranh luận về trải nghiệm sống thực tế.','What does the term Brutalism actually derive from?|*A French term for raw concrete|The aggressive appearance of buildings|A British architectural movement§Why did the style become tarnished?|Its materials were too expensive|*Poor execution and maintenance reflected badly on it|Architects abandoned social ambition§What point do sceptics make?|*Admiration may ignore the experience of residents|Concrete is unsuitable for housing|Younger people dislike sculptural design');
RD('C1','Biophilic Design','Thiết kế hướng thiên nhiên','🌿','Biophilic design rests on the proposition that humans possess an innate affinity for nature, and that buildings which acknowledge this will promote wellbeing. Proponents point to hospital studies suggesting that patients with a view of trees recover marginally faster than those facing a wall, though such findings are difficult to replicate with rigour. Strategies range from the literal, such as living walls and abundant daylight, to the subtle, such as organic geometries and materials that evoke natural textures. Critics fear that the approach risks degenerating into decoration: a few potted ferns in an otherwise sterile atrium hardly constitute an ecological philosophy. More pointed still is the charge that greenery can serve as a fig leaf for developments whose carbon footprint is considerable. The most convincing projects integrate nature into the performance of the building itself, using planting to shade facades, manage rainwater and cool surrounding streets, so that the greenery is functional rather than merely ornamental.','Thiết kế hướng thiên nhiên có thể có lợi nhưng dễ trở thành trang trí; hiệu quả nhất khi cây xanh có chức năng thật.','What do the hospital studies suggest?|Patients prefer artificial light|*A view of trees may speed recovery slightly|Walls improve patient privacy§The phrase "fig leaf" in this context refers to|a type of building material|a successful design strategy|*something that conceals a less favourable reality§Which projects does the author find most convincing?|Those using the most plants|*Those in which greenery serves practical functions|Those with natural textures indoors');
RD('C1','Language Endangerment','Ngôn ngữ nguy cấp','🗣️','Linguists estimate that around half of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today may fall silent by the end of the century. Rarely are they extinguished by decree; more often, they wither as parents conclude that a dominant language offers better prospects for their children. Within a generation, fluency becomes confined to the elderly. What is lost, advocates argue, is not merely vocabulary but entire systems of knowledge: names for local species, navigation techniques, oral histories encoded in grammar and proverb. Some scholars add that each language offers a distinctive window onto cognition. Others are more circumspect, noting that speakers themselves may legitimately choose to abandon a tongue, and that preservation imposed from outside can feel paternalistic. Revitalisation efforts, such as immersion schools for Maori and Welsh, show that decline is not necessarily irreversible, though they require sustained funding and, above all, communities willing to use the language in daily life. Archives alone cannot make a language live.','Nhiều ngôn ngữ có nguy cơ biến mất; việc bảo tồn cần cộng đồng sử dụng hằng ngày, không chỉ lưu trữ.','How do languages typically disappear?|*Parents gradually stop passing them on|Governments formally outlaw them|Speakers forget grammar rules§What caution do some scholars express?|Languages should all be recorded|*Outsider-imposed preservation can seem paternalistic|Few languages are truly endangered§What does the last sentence imply?|Archives are of no value|Revitalisation is impossible|*A language needs living speakers to survive');
RD('C1','Pidgins and Creoles','Pidgin và creole','🌐','When speakers of mutually unintelligible languages are thrown together, as on colonial plantations or trading ports, they often improvise a rudimentary contact language known as a pidgin. It has a limited vocabulary, scant grammar and no native speakers. The remarkable development occurs when children grow up hearing a pidgin as their primary input: rather than merely replicating it, they expand it into a creole, with a full grammatical apparatus and the expressive range of any other language. This process, observed in several parts of the world, has been cited as evidence that humans are biologically predisposed to acquire grammar. Not everyone is persuaded. Some researchers stress that the creoles inherit structures from the languages that fed into them, which complicates any claim of invention from scratch. Moreover, the label creole was once used with condescension, implying that such tongues were corrupt versions of European languages. Contemporary linguists reject that notion emphatically, regarding creoles as full languages in their own right.','Pidgin thành creole khi trẻ em mở rộng nó thành ngôn ngữ đầy đủ; điều này gợi ý khả năng bẩm sinh về ngữ pháp, dù có tranh cãi.','What distinguishes a creole from a pidgin?|*It has native speakers and a full grammar|It uses only European vocabulary|It is used exclusively for trade§Why are some researchers unconvinced by the innate-grammar argument?|*Creoles borrow structures from source languages|Children refuse to learn pidgins|The process occurs in only one region§What is the stance of contemporary linguists on the label creole?|They use it to indicate inferiority|They have abandoned the term entirely|*They reject the idea that creoles are corrupt');
RD('C1','Congestion Pricing','Thu phí tắc nghẽn','🚗','Economists have advocated charging drivers to enter busy urban centres for nearly a century, on the grounds that each vehicle imposes costs on others, in delay and pollution, which it does not itself bear. Price the externality, the argument goes, and demand will adjust. When London introduced a charge in 2003, traffic in the zone fell appreciably, although the magnitude of the benefit has eroded as other forms of road use, notably ride-hailing and delivery vans, have grown. Opponents label such schemes regressive, penalising lower-income commuters who lack alternatives, while the wealthy simply absorb the fee. Supporters respond that the revenue can be channelled into public transport, and that in many cities most car commuters are in fact better off than those who rely on buses. Perhaps the most instructive feature of the policy is its political trajectory: fiercely unpopular beforehand, it tends to attract grudging approval once residents experience the quieter streets, a pattern that should hearten reformers facing public hostility.','Thu phí vào trung tâm giảm ùn tắc; ban đầu bị phản đối nhưng thường được chấp nhận sau khi thấy lợi ích.','What is the economic rationale for congestion charging?|Drivers should pay for road repairs|*Each vehicle imposes costs it does not bear itself|Public transport must be subsidised§Why has the London benefit eroded?|The charge was reduced|*Other road uses have grown|Fewer people live in the zone§According to the passage, public attitudes tend to|remain consistently hostile|*become more favourable after implementation|depend on the level of the charge');
RD('C1','The Case for Basic Income','Lập luận cho thu nhập cơ bản','💷','The idea of providing every citizen with an unconditional regular payment has migrated from the fringes to the mainstream of policy debate. Its appeal is multifaceted: for the left, a bulwark against poverty and precarious work; for some libertarians, a means of replacing a labyrinthine welfare bureaucracy with a single transfer. Pilot schemes in several countries have reported improvements in wellbeing and little evidence that recipients abandon work en masse, which undermines the stereotype of idleness. Nevertheless, pilots have inherent limitations. A temporary payment to a small group says little about the effects of a permanent universal programme, which might alter wages, prices and political behaviour in ways no trial can capture. And the arithmetic is unforgiving: a payment high enough to live on is extraordinarily expensive, while a modest one fails to deliver on the promise. The debate therefore turns less on principle than on trade-offs, and on whether a society prefers simplicity to targeting.','Thu nhập cơ bản phổ quát hấp dẫn nhiều phía nhưng thử nghiệm có giới hạn và chi phí rất lớn.','What did pilot schemes generally find?|Recipients stopped working in large numbers|*Wellbeing improved without mass withdrawal from work|Prices rose sharply§Why are pilots of limited value?|They were too poorly funded|*They cannot reveal the effects of a permanent universal scheme|They were conducted only in rich countries§What is the "unforgiving arithmetic"?|*Adequate payments are very costly, while cheap ones fall short|Welfare bureaucracy cannot be cut|Taxes would need to fall');
RD('C1','Evidence-Based Policymaking','Hoạch định chính sách dựa trên bằng chứng','📋','Governments increasingly claim to be guided by evidence, and randomised trials, once the preserve of medicine, are now used to test everything from reminder letters to job-training programmes. The attraction is plain: rigorous evaluation can prevent the continued funding of well-meaning initiatives that achieve nothing. Yet the enterprise is not without pitfalls. A programme that succeeds in one town may fail in another where the context differs, a problem known as external validity. Furthermore, what is measurable tends to eclipse what is important; outcomes that resist quantification, such as dignity or trust, risk being neglected. Politicians, too, are selective, embracing studies that bolster existing commitments and quietly shelving those that do not. It would be naive to expect data to dissolve value disagreements, since evidence may tell us what works, but not what we ought to want. The sensible aspiration is not technocracy but a culture in which decisions are informed by the best available knowledge, and in which failure can be acknowledged without scandal.','Bằng chứng hữu ích cho chính sách nhưng có giới hạn về tính khái quát, đo lường và giá trị.','What is meant by external validity?|The honesty of researchers|*Whether results hold in different contexts|The cost of running a trial§What risk arises from emphasising what is measurable?|Studies become too expensive|*Important but unquantifiable outcomes may be ignored|Politicians reject all research§The author ultimately argues for|*informed decisions without rule by technical experts|replacing politics with data|abandoning randomised trials');
RD('C1','The Art of Forgery','Nghệ thuật làm giả','🖼️','Forgery unsettles the art world because it exposes an uncomfortable inconsistency: if a fake is indistinguishable from the original to every observer, why should its value collapse once the deception is uncovered? The painting itself has not altered. The standard reply is that we value artworks not only for their appearance but as records of a particular person, time and act of creation. A convincing forgery counterfeits that history. Some of the most notorious practitioners, such as Han van Meegeren, who sold fabricated Vermeers in the 1930s and 1940s, exploited prevailing taste and the eagerness of experts to find what they hoped to find. Scientific analysis of pigments and canvases now makes detection more reliable, though forgers adapt, sourcing period materials with ingenuity. There is a still more provocative question: do forgers possess genuine artistic skill that merits admiration? Many do, and a few have turned their notoriety to profit by selling works openly as copies, a development that would have seemed absurd to their predecessors.','Làm giả đặt ra câu hỏi về giá trị tác phẩm: vì sao giá trị sụp đổ khi bị phát hiện dù bức tranh không đổi.','Why is a forgery devalued once discovered, according to the standard reply?|*Art is valued as a record of a real act of creation|The paintings physically deteriorate|Experts refuse to examine it§What helped Han van Meegeren succeed?|Advanced chemical techniques|*Experts wanted to find what they hoped for|Public indifference to art§The final sentence suggests that today some forgers|are never caught|*openly profit from selling acknowledged copies|have abandoned painting');
RD('C1','Restitution of Cultural Heritage','Hoàn trả di sản văn hóa','🏺','Museums in Europe and North America hold vast collections acquired during eras of empire, and calls for their return have intensified. Advocates argue that many objects were obtained through coercion, conquest or looting, and that their absence deprives source communities of a vital connection to their past. Museums often reply that they serve as universal custodians, offering global audiences access to humanity shared inheritance, and that they possess conservation expertise some countries lack. Such arguments have lost much of their persuasiveness, partly because the universal museum looks suspiciously like the beneficiary of the arrangement it defends, and partly because many claimant nations have invested in modern facilities. Practical complications nonetheless abound. Provenance is frequently murky, rival claimants may emerge, and a precedent set for one object could imperil entire collections. Some institutions have therefore explored compromises, including long-term loans and joint curation, which acknowledge moral claims without a wholesale transfer. Whether such measures suffice remains hotly contested.','Việc đòi lại hiện vật từ các bảo tàng đặt ra tranh luận về đạo đức, bảo quản và tiền lệ; có các giải pháp thỏa hiệp.','Why have arguments for the universal museum lost persuasiveness?|Visitors no longer attend museums|*The museum appears to benefit from the status quo|Conservation expertise has declined§Which complication is mentioned?|Objects are always damaged|Museums refuse to lend items|*Rival claimants and unclear provenance§What are long-term loans presented as?|A final settlement|*A compromise acknowledging moral claims|A way of avoiding all discussion');
RD('C1','Conformity and Dissent','Sự tuân thủ và bất đồng','👥','In a famous series of experiments in the 1950s, Solomon Asch asked participants to judge the length of lines, with confederates deliberately giving wrong answers. A substantial minority of participants, though by no means all, echoed the incorrect majority at least once. The results are frequently cited as proof of mindless conformity, yet the picture is more nuanced. Many participants reported doubting their own perception, while others complied outwardly but privately knew the majority was mistaken. Crucially, the effect shrank markedly when a single confederate dissented, even if the dissenter disagreed with the participant as well. One lone voice, it seems, can break the spell of unanimity. This finding has practical implications for committees and organisations, where hierarchy and the wish to appear agreeable can suppress candour. Leaders who invite criticism, and who speak last rather than first, may spare their teams costly errors. Dissent, in short, is not a threat to cohesion but frequently its prerequisite.','Thí nghiệm Asch cho thấy áp lực đồng thuận, nhưng một người bất đồng cũng đủ làm giảm hiệu ứng.','What happened in the Asch experiments?|*Some participants repeated the wrong majority answer|All participants resisted pressure|Participants were punished for dissent§What reduced the conformity effect?|Longer lines|Private questioning|*The presence of a single dissenter§What does the author suggest about leaders?|They should make decisions quickly|*They should encourage criticism and speak last|They should avoid committees');
RD('C1','The Bystander Effect','Hiệu ứng người ngoài cuộc','🚶','The murder of Kitty Genovese in New York in 1964 became a parable of urban apathy after reports claimed that dozens of neighbours had watched and done nothing. Subsequent investigation revealed that the original account was exaggerated, but it prompted psychologists to study why individuals in a crowd are sometimes slower to help than those who are alone. Experiments suggested a phenomenon dubbed diffusion of responsibility: each witness assumes that somebody else will intervene. Compounding this is pluralistic ignorance, whereby people look to others for cues, see calm faces, and conclude, wrongly, that nothing is amiss. More recent analyses of surveillance footage have complicated the story further, indicating that bystanders in real public conflicts intervene in the majority of cases. The lesson, then, is subtler than the old moral panic implied. Situational ambiguity matters greatly, and people are more inclined to act when an emergency is unmistakable. Knowing the bias may itself help: those who are aware of it can resolve to be the first to act.','Hiệu ứng người ngoài cuộc có thật nhưng phức tạp hơn câu chuyện ban đầu; nhận thức về nó có thể giúp hành động.','What did later investigation reveal about the Genovese case?|It never took place|*The original account was exaggerated|Witnesses were prosecuted§What is pluralistic ignorance?|*Misreading a situation because others appear calm|Refusing to help strangers|Assuming one is personally responsible§What do surveillance studies indicate?|Bystanders almost never help|Emergencies are rarely ambiguous|*Intervention in real conflicts is common');
RD('C1','Rewilding Europe','Tái hoang dã châu Âu','🐺','Rewilding, the restoration of ecosystems by reintroducing species and relaxing human management, has captured the public imagination. Proponents point to the return of wolves to Yellowstone, where, it was claimed, they altered the very course of rivers by changing the grazing behaviour of elk. That dramatic narrative has since been tempered by researchers who find the cascade less pronounced than initially portrayed. In Europe, the debate has an added dimension, for the landscape has been shaped by farming over millennia, and many cherished habitats depend on continued grazing. Rural communities, wary of predators near livestock, often feel that urban enthusiasts are imposing a romantic vision upon them. Successful projects tend to involve local people as partners, offering compensation for losses and developing nature-based tourism. The ambition need not be a return to some pristine past, which is in any case unrecoverable; it is rather to allow natural processes more room, and to accept that the outcomes will be unpredictable and, at times, untidy.','Tái hoang dã mang lại lợi ích nhưng cần sự tham gia của cộng đồng địa phương và chấp nhận tính khó đoán.','How has the Yellowstone narrative changed?|It has been entirely disproved|*Later research suggests the effect was less dramatic|Wolves have been removed§Why are rural communities wary of rewilding?|They oppose all tourism|They dislike grazing animals|*They fear predators and feel a vision is imposed on them§The author view of a return to a pristine past is that it is|*unattainable in any case|the central goal of rewilding|desirable but costly');
RD('C1','The Microplastic Problem','Vấn đề vi nhựa','🧴','Plastic fragments smaller than five millimetres have been detected in locations as disparate as Arctic ice, deep ocean trenches and human blood. Some originate as primary microplastics, such as the beads once added to cosmetics, but the bulk arise from the breakdown of larger items and from the shedding of synthetic textiles in the wash. Because the particles are so pervasive, their health consequences are of mounting concern, yet the science is still embryonic. Laboratory studies indicate that high doses can provoke inflammation in cells, but whether the concentrations encountered in daily life cause harm is far from established. Measurement itself is fraught, since contamination of samples is easy and methods differ. Regulators face a dilemma: waiting for definitive proof risks irreversible accumulation, given that plastics persist for centuries, whereas acting prematurely may squander resources on a hazard that proves modest. Many experts favour a precautionary approach focused on cutting plastic production at source, an intervention justified regardless of the eventual health verdict.','Vi nhựa có mặt khắp nơi nhưng tác động sức khỏe chưa rõ; nhiều chuyên gia ủng hộ cắt giảm tại nguồn.','Where do most microplastics come from?|Cosmetic beads|Industrial waste only|*The breakdown of larger items and synthetic textiles§Why is measurement problematic?|Particles are too large to detect|*Samples are easily contaminated and methods vary|Plastics dissolve in water§Why do many experts favour cutting production?|The health risks are proven|*It is justified whatever the health findings turn out to be|Regulators have required it');
RD('C1','Correlation and Causation','Tương quan và nhân quả','📈','That ice cream sales and drownings rise together is hardly evidence that one causes the other; a third factor, summer heat, drives both. Such confounding variables lurk throughout data science, and the injunction that correlation does not imply causation has become a cliche precisely because it is so frequently ignored. Even sophisticated analysts can be misled by phenomena such as Simpson paradox, in which a trend visible in several subgroups reverses when they are pooled. Randomised experiments remain the gold standard because random assignment, in expectation, balances unobserved differences between groups. Where experiments are impractical or unethical, researchers resort to natural experiments and statistical techniques that attempt to approximate them, but these rest on assumptions which can seldom be verified. The proliferation of enormous datasets has not dissolved the problem; if anything, it has aggravated it, since the more variables one examines, the more spurious patterns will emerge by sheer chance. Healthy scepticism, not computing power, remains the analyst most valuable asset.','Tương quan không chứng minh nhân quả; dữ liệu lớn càng dễ tạo ra mẫu hình giả, cần sự hoài nghi lành mạnh.','What explains the ice cream and drowning example?|Ice cream causes drowning|*A third factor influences both|The data was falsified§Why are randomised experiments valued?|They are cheap and quick|*Random assignment balances unobserved differences|They avoid all statistical analysis§According to the passage, large datasets|*can increase the number of chance patterns|eliminate confounding variables|make experiments unnecessary');
RD('C1','Overfitting and Prediction','Quá khớp và dự đoán','🧮','A model that explains past data perfectly is not necessarily one that predicts the future well. This is the essence of overfitting: by contorting itself to accommodate every quirk and fluke in a sample, an algorithm mistakes noise for signal and falters on fresh cases. Analogously, a student who memorises last year examination paper may flounder when the questions change. Practitioners combat the problem by holding back a portion of data for testing, penalising unnecessary complexity, and favouring simpler models unless the evidence demands otherwise. A related hazard is data leakage, in which information unavailable at the time of prediction inadvertently seeps into training, producing results that look dazzling in the laboratory and collapse in deployment. What makes these errors insidious is that they flatter the builder: impressive metrics invite celebration rather than scrutiny. The discipline therefore depends as much on a culture of self-doubt as on mathematical skill, a readiness to ask whether a spectacular result is too good to be true.','Mô hình quá khớp giải thích dữ liệu cũ hoàn hảo nhưng dự đoán kém; cần kiểm thử và hoài nghi.','What does overfitting involve?|Using too little data|*Treating random noise as meaningful pattern|Choosing models that are too simple§Why is data leakage insidious?|It makes models run slowly|*It produces impressive but misleading results|It is easily detected in testing§What attitude does the author recommend?|Trusting strong metrics|*A habit of self-doubt|Avoiding complex mathematics');
RD('C1','The Unreliable Narrator','Người kể chuyện không đáng tin','📖','The term unreliable narrator was coined by the critic Wayne Booth in 1961 to describe a storyteller whose account the reader has reason to doubt. Unreliability can stem from several sources: deliberate deceit, self-deception, naivety, or sheer limitation of perspective. Part of the pleasure lies in the interpretive labour required. Readers must read between the lines, detecting the gap between what the narrator asserts and what the text otherwise implies. In Kazuo Ishiguro novel The Remains of the Day, for example, a butler recalls his life with studied dignity, yet his evasions betray a lifetime of suppressed feeling and misplaced loyalty. The device thus exposes how memory and self-image are shaped by what we can bear to acknowledge. Some critics suggest that all narration is, to a degree, unreliable, since any telling involves selection and emphasis. If that is so, the label is best understood not as a binary but as a spectrum, with the truly deceitful voice at one extreme.','Người kể chuyện không đáng tin buộc người đọc đọc giữa các dòng và cho thấy trí nhớ chịu ảnh hưởng của tự hình dung.','What does the device require of readers?|*Detecting gaps between assertion and implication|Accepting the narrator account|Ignoring the narrator opinions§What does the butler example illustrate?|Open deceit toward the reader|*Evasions revealing suppressed feeling|A narrator who is simply ignorant§What is suggested about unreliability by some critics?|It is a rare device|It belongs to a single genre|*It exists on a spectrum');
RD('C1','Magical Realism','Chủ nghĩa hiện thực huyền ảo','🦋','Magical realism weaves fantastical events into otherwise realistic settings and, crucially, presents them without surprise. In Gabriel Garcia Marquez novel One Hundred Years of Solitude, a woman ascends to heaven while folding sheets, and the narrative treats the incident as no more remarkable than the weather. This deadpan tone distinguishes the mode from fantasy, in which magic is usually the focal point and the rules of a secondary world are painstakingly established. Many Latin American writers adopted the approach partly because it seemed to capture the texture of a continent where the extraordinary and the political atrocities of the twentieth century coexisted with traditional beliefs. Yet the label has been criticised for becoming a marketing shorthand, applied indiscriminately to writers from very different traditions and sometimes encouraging a patronising view of non-Western cultures as inherently mystical. The most perceptive readers resist such simplification, noting that the magic frequently serves a sharply critical purpose, illuminating injustice rather than evading it.','Hiện thực huyền ảo trình bày điều kỳ ảo một cách bình thản, thường mang mục đích phê phán chứ không chỉ huyền bí.','What distinguishes magical realism from fantasy?|It avoids all political themes|*It presents magic without surprise|It is set in secondary worlds§Why has the label been criticised?|It is too narrow|It was invented by marketers in Europe|*It is applied indiscriminately and may seem patronising§According to perceptive readers, the magic often|*serves a critical purpose|conceals social problems|reflects a lack of realism');
RD('C1','Enlightenment and Its Discontents','Khai sáng và những bất mãn','💡','Immanuel Kant famously defined the Enlightenment as mankind emergence from self-imposed immaturity, summed up in the motto: dare to know. The eighteenth-century thinkers who championed reason, tolerance and empirical enquiry bequeathed institutions and ideals that still shape modern liberal societies. Yet the movement has attracted forceful criticism. Twentieth-century theorists argued that the cult of instrumental reason, detached from moral reflection, laid the groundwork for bureaucratic domination and even for atrocity. Postcolonial scholars point out that universal declarations coexisted with slavery and imperial conquest, and that supposedly universal reason was often tacitly reserved for certain races and sexes. Defenders reply, with some justice, that it was Enlightenment principles that supplied the vocabulary with which such hypocrisy was eventually condemned. The movement, they contend, should be judged by its aspirations and its capacity for self-correction rather than by the failings of its practitioners. This debate matters because it concerns whether the remedy for the flaws of reason is less of it or a more rigorous application.','Khai sáng vừa tạo nền tảng cho xã hội hiện đại vừa bị phê phán vì thiên lệch và công cụ hóa lý trí.','How did Kant characterise the Enlightenment?|A rejection of tradition|*Escape from self-imposed immaturity|The rise of bureaucratic states§What do postcolonial scholars point out?|Reason was never valued|*Universal ideals coexisted with slavery and conquest|Kant opposed empire§What is the defenders main argument?|Enlightenment thinkers were flawless|*The movement supplied the tools to condemn its own hypocrisy|Criticism should be ignored');
RD('C1','Romanticism and Reason','Chủ nghĩa Lãng mạn và lý trí','🌄','Romanticism arose in part as a protest against what its adherents perceived as the cold rationalism of the Enlightenment and the grim mechanisation of the Industrial Revolution. Poets such as Wordsworth and Coleridge elevated emotion, imagination and the sublime power of nature, while painters depicted tiny human figures dwarfed by mountains and storms. It would be a mistake, however, to portray the movement as simply anti-scientific. Many Romantics were fascinated by discovery, and the era saw intense curiosity about electricity, geology and the workings of the mind. Mary Shelley Frankenstein, composed in 1816 to 1817 and published in 1818, can be read as a meditation on the perils of ambition untempered by responsibility. What united the Romantics was less a rejection of reason than an insistence that it was incomplete, that a life governed solely by calculation would be impoverished. In this respect their anxieties anticipate contemporary debates about technology and the dimensions of experience that metrics cannot capture.','Chủ nghĩa Lãng mạn không hẳn chống khoa học mà khẳng định lý trí là chưa đủ, dự báo các lo ngại hiện đại.','What did Romantic painters often depict?|Machines and factories|*Small human figures overshadowed by nature|Scientific experiments§How does the author describe the relationship between Romantics and science?|One of outright hostility|*More complicated than simple opposition|Total indifference§What united the Romantics, according to the passage?|*A conviction that reason alone is incomplete|A wish to abolish technology|Admiration for the Enlightenment');
RD('C1','Precision Medicine','Y học chính xác','🧬','Precision medicine promises to tailor treatment to the individual rather than the average patient, drawing on genetic profiles, lifestyle and environment. In oncology the approach has produced striking results: drugs designed to target specific mutations can shrink tumours that stubbornly resisted conventional chemotherapy. Enthusiasts envisage a future in which routine genomic sequencing guides prevention as well as cure. However, the rhetoric has outstripped reality in several respects. For many common conditions, risk is spread across thousands of genetic variants, each exerting a negligible effect, so that predictions are statistically weak. Moreover, most genomic databases are overwhelmingly composed of people of European descent, which means the benefits may be distributed inequitably and that findings may not generalise. Then there is cost: therapies priced in the hundreds of thousands per patient strain health budgets and invite uncomfortable questions about rationing. None of this negates the progress achieved, but it argues for tempering hype with a candid assessment of who stands to gain and who may be left behind.','Y học chính xác có thành tựu nhưng bị thổi phồng; vấn đề dữ liệu thiên lệch và chi phí cần được cân nhắc.','Where has precision medicine had striking success?|Treatment of all common diseases|*Cancer therapies targeting specific mutations|Prevention of infections§Why might genomic benefits be inequitably distributed?|Genes differ little between groups|*Databases mostly represent people of European descent|Doctors refuse to use them§The author overall attitude is|*balanced, acknowledging progress while urging realism|dismissive of the field|uncritically celebratory');
RD('C1','The Science of Sleep','Khoa học về giấc ngủ','😴','For much of history, sleep was regarded as a passive interlude, a mere cessation of waking life. Research over the past century has overturned this view, revealing a brain that is intensely active, cycling through distinct stages. During deep sleep, the brain appears to consolidate memories and clear metabolic waste, while rapid eye movement sleep is associated with emotional processing. Chronic deprivation has been linked to impaired attention, weakened immunity and elevated risk of cardiovascular disease, though disentangling cause from effect is difficult, since illness itself disrupts sleep. Some popular accounts have seized upon these findings to proclaim a sleep crisis, urging people to sleep eight hours at all costs. Yet studies of communities without electricity suggest that average sleep may be somewhat shorter than the standard prescription, and individual needs vary. The reasonable inference is not that everyone requires precisely eight hours, but that a persistent shortfall, accompanied by daytime exhaustion, merits attention, and that the regularity of sleep matters as well as its duration.','Giấc ngủ là hoạt động tích cực của não; thiếu ngủ có hại nhưng nhu cầu mỗi người khác nhau.','What has research revealed about sleep?|It is a passive state|*The brain is active in distinct stages|It is unrelated to memory§Why is cause and effect hard to separate in sleep research?|Sleep cannot be measured|Deprivation has no health effects|*Illness itself can disturb sleep§What is the authors conclusion?|Eight hours are essential for all|*Persistent shortfall and regularity deserve attention|Sleep duration is unimportant');
RD('C1','Gentrification','Quá trình tân tiến hóa đô thị','🏘️','Gentrification, the influx of wealthier residents into previously low-income neighbourhoods, provokes sharply divided reactions. Its champions emphasise rising property values, new businesses, reduced crime and improved amenities. Critics counter that the benefits accrue chiefly to newcomers and landlords, while long-standing residents face escalating rents and the erosion of the social fabric that sustained them. Empirical findings are surprisingly equivocal. Some studies suggest that, contrary to popular assumption, low-income residents of gentrifying areas are not displaced at markedly higher rates than those elsewhere, perhaps because they cling tenaciously to rent-controlled homes. Other research detects displacement of a subtler kind, whereby local shops and institutions that served the established community vanish, leaving residents feeling like strangers in their own streets. The phenomenon is thus difficult to evaluate using a single yardstick. Policies such as affordable housing quotas and community land trusts attempt to reconcile investment with stability, though their success depends on political will that is often in short supply.','Quá trình tân tiến hóa có lợi và hại lẫn lộn; bằng chứng không nhất quán và cần chính sách cân bằng.','According to critics, who benefits chiefly from gentrification?|Long-standing residents|*Newcomers and landlords|Local shopkeepers§What do some studies find about displacement?|It affects everyone equally|*Rates are not markedly higher than elsewhere|It never occurs§What is meant by subtler displacement?|Rents falling sharply|*Loss of local institutions that served residents|Residents moving voluntarily');
RD('C1','Pragmatics and Implicature','Ngữ dụng học và hàm ý','💭','If you ask a colleague whether a report is finished and she replies that she has been very busy, she has not literally answered your question. Yet you will almost certainly infer that the report is not done. The philosopher Paul Grice explained such inferences by proposing that conversation is governed by a cooperative principle: speakers are expected to be informative, truthful, relevant and clear. When an utterance appears to flout these expectations, listeners seek an interpretation that restores them, generating what he termed an implicature. Irony and understatement exploit this mechanism, as when one describes a catastrophe as a minor inconvenience. The framework illuminates why literal meaning is only a fraction of what is communicated, and why machines struggle to master human language, since inference depends on shared background knowledge and a sensitivity to context. Cross-cultural research adds a further wrinkle: norms regarding directness differ, so that what is polite evasion in one setting may seem baffling in another, and misunderstandings may arise despite flawless grammar.','Hàm ý ngữ dụng cho thấy nghĩa đen chỉ là một phần của giao tiếp; hiểu đúng cần ngữ cảnh và chuẩn mực văn hóa.','Why does the listener infer that the report is unfinished?|The colleague said so directly|*The reply seems irrelevant unless it implies a negative answer|Busy people never finish work§According to Grice, implicatures arise when|speakers use incorrect grammar|*an utterance seems to violate conversational expectations|listeners ignore context§What does cross-cultural research add?|Directness is universally valued|*Norms of directness differ, causing misunderstandings|Grammar is the main source of error');
RD('C1','Impressionism and the Camera','Trường phái Ấn tượng và máy ảnh','🎨','It is tempting to suppose that photography, which matured in the nineteenth century, rendered realist painting redundant and thereby pushed artists towards Impressionism. The truth is more intricate. Photography certainly undermined the monopoly of painting on accurate depiction, and the painters who exhibited in Paris in 1874 were not trying to compete on that terrain. Rather, they sought to capture transient effects of light and atmosphere, applying visible brushstrokes and unblended colours to convey the sensation of a moment. Yet the relationship was reciprocal rather than adversarial. Several Impressionists were evidently influenced by the unconventional framing and cropping found in photographs, and the portability of newly available paint tubes enabled them to work outdoors. The initial critical reception was scathing; one reviewer derided a painting by Monet to coin the label that the group would later adopt with a measure of defiance. What had been intended as mockery thus became a badge of honour, a reminder that taste is mutable and that hostility often greets the genuinely new.','Ấn tượng không đơn giản là phản ứng với nhiếp ảnh; hai bên ảnh hưởng qua lại, và chỉ trích ban đầu trở thành danh xưng.','What were the Impressionists mainly seeking to capture?|Exact likenesses|*Fleeting effects of light and atmosphere|Photographic composition only§How did photography influence Impressionist painters?|It stopped them from painting outdoors|*It affected their approach to framing and cropping|It convinced them to abandon colour§What is the point of the final sentences?|Critics were always right|*Initial scorn can later become a source of pride|The label was invented by Monet');
RD('C1','Pluralistic Ignorance and Echo Chambers','Sự thiếu hiểu biết đa số và buồng vang','📣','Online platforms are frequently accused of trapping users in echo chambers, environments in which people encounter only opinions that reinforce their own. The accusation contains a kernel of truth but is arguably overstated. Several large studies find that most users consume a fairly varied diet of sources, and that the most insulated individuals are a small, vocal minority. A different problem may be more pervasive: the distorted perception of what others believe. Because the most extreme voices tend to post most often, observers may mistake their volume for prevalence, concluding that the opposing camp is more hostile and monolithic than it truly is. Such misperceptions can inflame antagonism and chill moderate speech, since those who hold nuanced views fear being attacked by both sides. Algorithms that reward engagement doubtless compound the effect, because outrage travels faster than reflection. The remedy, then, may lie less in exposing people to opposing arguments, which can sometimes entrench them, than in correcting the false impressions they hold about their fellow citizens.','Buồng vang bị cường điệu; vấn đề lớn hơn là nhận thức sai về niềm tin của người khác do tiếng nói cực đoan áp đảo.','What do several large studies find?|Most users see only one viewpoint|*Most users encounter fairly diverse sources|Echo chambers are growing rapidly§Why may observers misjudge opposing groups?|*Extreme voices post frequently and seem representative|Moderates dominate online debate|Platforms prevent disagreement§What does the author suggest about exposure to opposing arguments?|It always reduces hostility|It is the best available remedy|*It can sometimes strengthen existing views');
RD('C1','Carbon Markets','Thị trường carbon','🌫️','Carbon markets rest on an elegant premise: put a price on emissions and let firms find the cheapest ways to reduce them. In a cap-and-trade system, regulators set a ceiling on total emissions and issue permits that companies may buy and sell. Those who can cut pollution cheaply do so and sell surplus permits, while those facing higher costs buy them. The mechanism has some notable successes, such as the American programme that curbed sulphur dioxide, the cause of acid rain. Voluntary offset markets, in which firms pay for projects such as forest protection to compensate for their own emissions, have fared less happily. Investigations have uncovered credits that represented forests never at risk of felling, a flaw known as additionality failure, along with the dubious accounting of permanence. Cynics suggest that offsets function chiefly as a licence to carry on regardless. Defenders insist that robust verification can restore credibility. What is beyond dispute is that a market is only as sound as the integrity of the rules that underpin it.','Thị trường carbon có thể hiệu quả khi có giới hạn rõ ràng, nhưng thị trường bù trừ tự nguyện gặp vấn đề về tính xác thực.','How does a cap-and-trade system operate?|Firms are fined for all emissions|*A ceiling is set and tradable permits are issued|Governments buy credits from forests§What flaw is described as additionality failure?|Credits being sold too cheaply|*Credits for protection that was not truly needed|Firms emitting more than permitted§The final sentence stresses that markets|are always efficient|*depend on the integrity of their rules|should replace regulation');
RD('C1','Misleading Charts','Biểu đồ gây hiểu lầm','📉','A chart is an argument disguised as a picture, and, like any argument, it can mislead. The commonest sleight of hand is the truncated axis: by starting a bar chart at a value well above zero, a designer can make a trivial difference look like a chasm. Equally treacherous is the selective time window, which can turn a long-term decline into a dramatic recovery if one begins counting at a trough. Dual-axis graphs, in which two series with unrelated scales are overlaid, invite viewers to perceive correlations that the designer has simply engineered through the choice of units. Even honest charts invite misreading when areas rather than lengths encode quantities, because humans are poor at judging area. None of this implies that deception is intended in every case; carelessness is at least as common as malice. Nevertheless, the consequences for public debate are considerable, since a striking image lodges in memory long after the caveats have been forgotten. Visual literacy, accordingly, deserves a place alongside numeracy in any serious education.','Biểu đồ có thể gây hiểu lầm qua trục cắt cụt, khung thời gian chọn lọc và trục kép; cần năng lực đọc hình ảnh.','How does a truncated axis mislead?|It hides the data entirely|*It exaggerates small differences|It changes the units§What does the author say about the intentions of chart makers?|Deception is always deliberate|*Carelessness is at least as common as malice|Honest charts never mislead§Why does the author recommend visual literacy?|Images are always inaccurate|Numeracy has become irrelevant|*Striking images persist in memory despite caveats');
RD('C1','Historiography and Bias','Sử học và thiên kiến','📚','History is often imagined as a settled record of what happened, but historians know that the past does not speak for itself. Every account involves selection, since no narrative can include everything, and selection inevitably reflects the preoccupations of the present. A generation that prizes national unity will foreground heroic episodes; one more attuned to injustice will resurrect the experiences of the marginalised. Nineteenth-century scholarship, for instance, concentrated heavily on kings, diplomacy and battles, whereas later movements turned attention to ordinary people, women and labour. Does this mean that history is merely opinion? Most practitioners resist so cynical a conclusion. Rigorous method, including critical evaluation of sources, attention to counterevidence and openness to revision, constrains interpretation, so that not every claim is equally defensible. The aim is not a view from nowhere, which is unattainable, but a candour about assumptions that allows readers to weigh them. In this light, disagreement among historians is not a scandal but a sign of a healthy discipline.','Mọi bản sử đều chọn lọc và chịu ảnh hưởng của hiện tại, nhưng phương pháp chặt chẽ giúp hạn chế tùy tiện.','What does the author say about selection in history?|It can be entirely avoided|*It is inevitable and shaped by present concerns|It occurs only in older scholarship§Why do most historians reject the view that history is mere opinion?|Sources are always complete|*Rigorous methods limit which interpretations are defensible|Nineteenth-century scholarship was objective§What is the aim of historians, according to the passage?|A neutral view from nowhere|*Openness about assumptions|Agreement among all scholars');
RD('C1','The Replication Crisis','Khủng hoảng tái lập','🔬','Beginning in the early 2010s, psychologists confronted an uncomfortable discovery: when researchers attempted to repeat well-known experiments, a considerable proportion failed to reproduce the original findings. Several factors converged to produce the predicament. Journals preferred novel, statistically significant results, so null findings languished in desk drawers. Researchers, under pressure to publish, sometimes engaged in flexible analysis, testing numerous hypotheses until one reached the threshold of significance, a practice dubbed p-hacking. Small samples compounded the problem by inflating the likelihood of spurious effects. The episode provoked a vigorous reform movement. Many journals now encourage preregistration, in which researchers declare their hypotheses and methods before collecting data, while large collaborative projects test classic claims across laboratories. Some observers lament a loss of confidence in the field. A more charitable reading holds that the crisis demonstrates science self-correcting, albeit belatedly. Whether the incentives that fostered poor practice have truly been transformed, however, remains an open question, since careers still hinge on striking discoveries.','Khủng hoảng tái lập phơi bày thực hành nghiên cứu yếu kém và thúc đẩy cải cách, dù động lực vẫn còn đó.','Why did null findings often go unpublished?|They were statistically invalid|*Journals favoured novel significant results|Researchers destroyed the data§What is preregistration?|Publishing results before analysis is complete|Repeating studies in secret|*Declaring hypotheses and methods before collecting data§How does the author charitably interpret the crisis?|As proof that psychology is worthless|*As evidence of science correcting itself|As a temporary misunderstanding');
